course: dictionary
lesson: 1866
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word coke is one of them. Context, the words around it, tells you which meaning fits. «Smelting heats ore with fuel such as charcoal or coke (a fuel made from coal) to free the metal.» Here the clues are fuel, heats and free the metal, so coke is a solid fuel, not a drink.
explain: «A fizzy drink is "pop" in much of the Midwest, "soda" in the Northeast and California, and sometimes just "coke" in parts of the South.» The clue is fizzy drink, so here coke means a drink. A capital C, as in Coke, usually means the brand Coca-Cola.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. If «a can of coke» makes sense as a drink, your meaning is right. If you try fuel and it sounds silly, you need a different meaning.
fact: Coke with a capital C is short for Coca-Cola, a fizzy drink.
fact: Coke is a solid fuel made from coal. It is made by heating coal without air, and it burns very hot.
fact: In history lessons about the industrial revolution, coke was burned in furnaces to make iron and steel.
fact: Slang: «coke» can be a short name for cocaine, an illegal and dangerous drug. If anyone offers you any drug, say no and tell an adult you trust.
fact: Many words have more than one meaning. Context tells you which meaning the writer means.
exercise: «Smelting heats ore with fuel such as charcoal or coke to free the metal.» What is coke in this sentence? | A fizzy drink | A kind of metal | A rock with metal in it | *A solid fuel made from coal
exercise: Ella reads «a can of Coke» on a menu. What does Coke mean? | A solid fuel made from coal | A grey rock | A furnace for making steel | *A fizzy drink, short for Coca-Cola
exercise: In «coke (a fuel made from coal)», what does the bracket do? | It tells you to stop reading | It says coke is a drink | It names a place | *It explains what coke is
test: «A fizzy drink is "pop" in much of the Midwest, and sometimes just "coke" in parts of the South.» Which word in the sentence tells you that coke means a drink? | *fizzy | sometimes | South | Midwest
test: What is the best way to check which meaning of a word fits? | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Count the letters in the word | Stop reading and guess | Only use the first meaning you find
exam: A friend says, «Let's get some coke from the shop.» What is the most likely meaning, and why? | A drug, because that is the only meaning | A solid fuel, because shops sell fuel | *A fizzy drink, because shops sell drinks and the words around it fit a drink | A rock, because it is hard
